WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.531
A worker thread spins in a loop until a plain boolean called stop turns true.

2
00:00:07.531 --> 00:00:10.543
A second later, the main thread sets it to true.

3
00:00:10.767 --> 00:00:14.362
On JDK 21 or 25, the worker never stops.

4
00:00:14.362 --> 00:00:17.777
Not after a second, not after an hour.

5
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.438
The usual story blames the CPU cache.

6
00:00:20.438 --> 00:00:25.116
Each core keeps its own copy of stop, and the worker's copy goes stale.

7
00:00:25.116 --> 00:00:27.620
Here is what the hardware really does.

8
00:00:28.433 --> 00:00:32.593
Each core has registers, a small store buffer, and its own cache.

9
00:00:32.593 --> 00:00:35.600
Below them sits a shared cache and main memory.

10
00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:37.904
Right now, stop is false everywhere.

11
00:00:38.133 --> 00:00:39.793
The main thread writes true.

12
00:00:39.793 --> 00:00:41.986
The core does not wait for the cache.

13
00:00:41.986 --> 00:00:46.431
It puts the write in its store buffer, and runs on to its next instruction.

14
00:00:46.633 --> 00:00:49.668
For a moment, only this core knows about the write.

15
00:00:49.668 --> 00:00:52.584
That is how a store buffer lets a core run ahead.

16
00:00:52.584 --> 00:00:57.107
A later read of another field can finish before this write leaves the queue.

17
00:00:57.333 --> 00:00:59.716
But the queue drains within nanoseconds.

18
00:00:59.716 --> 00:01:01.443
The write lands in the cache.

19
00:01:01.667 --> 00:01:03.728
Caches run a coherence protocol.

20
00:01:03.728 --> 00:01:08.432
When one core writes, the other cores' copies are updated or thrown away.

21
00:01:08.432 --> 00:01:11.073
So core 0's cache soon says true as well.

22
00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:12.454
The caches agree.

23
00:01:12.454 --> 00:01:14.899
No cache keeps an old value forever.

24
00:01:14.899 --> 00:01:17.208
So why does the worker still spin?

25
00:01:17.433 --> 00:01:18.944
Look at its register.

26
00:01:18.944 --> 00:01:22.542
The compiled loop read stop once, into a register.

27
00:01:22.542 --> 00:01:24.557
It never reads memory again.

28
00:01:25.467 --> 00:01:31.127
After the loop has run many thousands of times, the JIT compiler turns it into machine code.

29
00:01:31.333 --> 00:01:34.420
C2 sees that nothing inside the loop writes stop.

30
00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:38.579
So it reads stop once, before the loop, and runs the rest forever.

31
00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:39.796
That is allowed.

32
00:01:39.796 --> 00:01:44.898
For a plain field, the memory model gives no promise about another thread's write.

33
00:01:44.898 --> 00:01:48.569
So the compiler may assume that no other thread changes it.

34
00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:50.833
You can check where the bug lives.

35
00:01:50.833 --> 00:01:53.762
Turn the JIT off with one flag, and run it again.

36
00:01:53.762 --> 00:01:56.991
The caches are the same, and the worker stops at once.

37
00:01:57.900 --> 00:01:59.376
The fix is one word.

38
00:01:59.376 --> 00:02:01.369
Declare the field volatile.

39
00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:06.693
The JIT can't keep a volatile field in a register, or move its read out of the loop.

40
00:02:06.693 --> 00:02:08.269
Every pass reads it again.

41
00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:11.716
Now each pass of the loop goes to the cache.

42
00:02:11.716 --> 00:02:16.473
The cache already says true, so the worker sees main's write, and the loop ends.

43
00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:22.125
And the memory model guarantees that main's volatile write becomes visible to the worker.
